CV LABELLING TOOL USAGE GUIDE:
First, ensure you have the sam3 weights installed (they are on NAS under MTR Inspection/MTR_CV/detection_ws/core/sam3). 

Loading Files

Launc the gui and press File -> "Open stereo folders..." (left/right) / "Open folder..." (Ctrl+Shift+O, one side only). If you want to use rerun you must also open a .rrd file (File -> "Open rerun file..." or the "🎬 Rerun viewer / map..." button) and an image pose database with image <-> odom data (File -> "Open pose database..."). After opening an image folder(s), you can "File -> Load annotations file..." (Ctrl+I) to load in a mscoco format annotations file. 

Adding / Deleting Bboxes and Segmentation

Enter add bbox mode by pressing "A" key. You can delete a bbox with Backspace or "D" key. You can click and drag to draw a bounding box, then enter 0-9 and press Enter key to spawn the bbox (if a category is already preselected / last-used, the box spawns immediately with no typing). You can select a bbox by clicking on it (Shift+click for multi-select, Ctrl+A for all boxes) and then press "Run SAM3 (all)" to segment every bbox on the frame, or "Re-seg sel (R)" to re-segment only the selected bbox(es). If you just highlight categories without drawing bboxes, use "Autolabel frame" / "Autolabel ALL frames" (with the sam3 detector): SAM3 then runs detection+segmentation with those category names as the prompt. SAM3 can also take in positive/negative points for fine grained Promptable Visual Segmentation, working especially well for small objects: toggle "🎯 Add points", left-click for positive points, right-click for negative points, then press "▶ Segment points" (Enter accepts, Esc cancels).

How to label data:
You need to label data in a way that the detection model outputs results which are beneficial for grounding. This means:
1) Do not label objects too far away (within ~3m by naked eye)
2) do not label occluded objects which only can be seen partially.
3) do not label objects behind glass / do not label reflections

Waypoint Selection:
For selecting waypoints, you can either: "Draw bboxes on an image" or click "✔ Mark as annotated". Then, switch to the embedded rerun viewer with View -> "Switch to Rerun waypoint view" (switch back with View -> "Switch back to image view"). This needs the .rrd file and IMAGE POSE DATABASE loaded. You can click on "🗺 Show annotated in Rerun" button to view the waypoints inside rerun. "◀ Prev annotated / Next annotated ▶" jump between annotated frames (boxed or marked). To run interval selection, see image_selection/sample_images_along_trajectory.py. You need to combine the json result with the ms coco json produced by the gui. use 09b_split_and_combine.py to do that (pretty easy, just merge the annotated_timestamp list in the json, need to manually add in the left and right side in the image ids in the database, merging the timestamp just gives 1 timestamp per pair). 

Configuration:
You can edit important configurations via File -> "Config settings..." (Ctrl+G) or the "⚙ Config" corner button, and save your settings ("Save...") for quick future loading ("Load from file..."). 

SAM3 propagation / Interpolation:
There are many frames where an object is stationary / moving slowly. SAM3 can take in bbox exemplars as prompts to track an object between keyframes, reducing human effort. Interpolation is useful when camera motion is linear / moving in a constant direction or stationary. Mark the start and end keyframe for SAM3 propagation ("Propagate →" / "⇉ Propagate all keyframes") / Interpolation ("Interpolate (I)" / "⇉ Interpolate all keyframes"); It will run between those keyframes. For Interpolation, you need to add the track ids of objects of interest in the start and end keyframe so they match up (boxes are paired by (category, track_id) first, with a nearest-center fallback). This is useful for instance if you have a large dataset and only want to label a small set of data and propagate your labels to the rest of the dataset. 
